---
description: Conventions for Unity Editor scripts and keeping editor-only code out of player builds
globs: **/Assets/**/*.cs
alwaysApply: false
---

# When this rule applies

- For runtime scripts, apply only `Keeping editor code out of builds`.
- For scripts under an `Editor/` folder or scripts defining editor tooling, apply all sections.

# When to use an Editor script

- Prefer `NaughtyAttributes` on runtime `MonoBehaviour` / `ScriptableObject` types for simple inspector behaviour (buttons, show/hide fields, ranges).
- Create an Editor script only when UI is non-trivial (multi-object workflows, custom layouts, batch tools, `MenuItem` utilities).

# Editor script conventions

- Place files under `Assets/**/Editor/` (e.g. `Assets/Scripts/Editor/`, `Assets/Scripts/Utils/Editor/`).
- Use `namespace Nex.*.Editor` (e.g. `Nex.Utils.Editor`, `Nex.Dev.Editor`).
- Use `[CustomEditor]`, `[CustomPropertyDrawer]`, or `[MenuItem("Nex/...")]` as appropriate.
- Record undo for asset changes: `Undo.RecordObject(...)` before modifying serialized objects.
- Fix Rider and Unity warnings/errors in changed editor code.

# Keeping editor code out of builds

- `UnityEditor` APIs do not exist in player builds. Code that uses them goes under an `Editor/` folder or inside `#if UNITY_EDITOR`; otherwise the Editor compiles but the build fails.
- NaughtyAttributes attributes (`[Button]`, `[ShowIf]`, ...) are safe in runtime scripts; only their drawers are editor-only.
- NUnit tests live in a test assembly (asmdef referencing the Test Framework), never in runtime scripts.
- Do not run a local build to check this unless asked; it is very slow.
